    <!--
        Intuvance fork.

        The groupId, version and metadata below are the only divergence from upstream. Two things
        must NOT change, and are deliberately left exactly as upstream has them:

          * <artifactId>initializer</artifactId> — omod/src/main/resources/config.xml filters the
            OpenMRS module id from ${project.parent.artifactId}. Rename the artifact and the
            installed module id changes with it, at which point this module sits ALONGSIDE the
            upstream initializer instead of replacing it. Two Initializer modules on the classpath
            is a worse failure than the bug this fork fixes, because whichever one the classloader
            happens to resolve wins silently.

          * <artifactId>initializer-omod</artifactId> and the api-* artifactIds — so that a
            consumer only has to change the groupId, and so the omod's own
            ${project.parent.artifactId}-api dependency references keep resolving.

        The version carries an -intuvance qualifier so the provenance is visible in OpenMRS'
        startup logs, which print the module id and version at boot. Without it a patched module
        reports itself as plain 2.12.1 and there is no way to tell from the logs which Initializer
        is actually running.
    -->
    <groupId>io.github.intuvance</groupId>
    <artifactId>initializer</artifactId>
    <version>2.12.1-intuvance.1</version>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>
    <name>OpenMRS Initializer (Intuvance)</name>
    <description>Intuvance fork of the OpenMRS Initializer module. The Initializer processes the content of the configuration folder when it is found inside OpenMRS application data directory. This fork changes one behaviour, applied in four line processors: a content package line that declares no concept mappings, concept set members or answers, descriptions, or drug reference maps no longer deletes what the concept or drug already had. Upstream cleared each of those collections before reading the line, which silently destroyed a pre-populated database's terminology on its first boot. A line that does declare them still replaces them wholesale, so the upstream "the CSV wins" semantics are unchanged.</description>

